description: Global rules for the US-based premium technology agency website
alwaysApply: true
---

# PROJECT

We are building a premium US-based technology/software agency website.

The website should combine the visual quality of a modern media/creative agency with the credibility and clarity of a premium technology company.

Primary objectives:

- Strong organic SEO
- US-market positioning
- Premium visual design
- High conversion
- Excellent UX
- Excellent performance
- Fully responsive experience
- Smooth but performance-conscious interactions


# TECHNOLOGY

Use:

- Next.js
- App Router
- JavaScript
- MongoDB
- Mongoose
- Tailwind CSS or well-structured CSS when appropriate

IMPORTANT:

JavaScript ONLY.

Never introduce TypeScript.

Do not create:

- .ts files
- .tsx files
- TypeScript configuration
- TypeScript-specific application code

Do not convert JavaScript files to TypeScript.


# WEBSITE ARCHITECTURE

IMPORTANT:

This must NOT be a fully dynamic website.

Use static/server-rendered content whenever possible.

Keep these primarily static/server-rendered:

- Homepage
- About
- Services
- Service pages
- Industries
- Industry pages
- Process
- Technology/capabilities
- Navigation
- Footer
- Contact page content
- Privacy Policy
- Terms

Use MongoDB/backend only where persistent data is genuinely required.

Potential database-driven content:

- Blog posts
- Blog categories
- Blog authors
- Contact submissions
- Admin users
- Case studies only if there is a genuine operational requirement

Do NOT put normal marketing content into MongoDB simply to make it editable.

Do NOT create a CMS for the entire website.


# SEO

SEO is a core architectural requirement.

SEO must be considered from the beginning of every phase.

Never build a page first and plan to "SEO it later."

For every important page consider:

- Search intent
- Primary keyword
- Secondary keywords
- URL structure
- H1
- Heading hierarchy
- Metadata
- Canonical URL
- Internal links
- Structured data
- Crawlability
- Indexability
- Performance
- Mobile usability

The website targets the United States market.

Use natural US business language.

Never keyword stuff.


# SEO RULES

Do not use:

- Keyword stuffing
- Hidden text
- Hidden keywords
- Doorway pages
- Duplicate location pages
- AI-generated spam
- Fake reviews
- Fake testimonials
- Fake clients
- Fake awards
- Fake statistics
- Unsupported claims
- Manipulative structured data

Content must be useful to people first and search engines second.


# URL STRUCTURE

Use clean, permanent URLs.

Examples:

/services/web-development/
/services/mobile-app-development/
/services/custom-software-development/
/industries/real-estate/
/work/project-name/
/blog/article-name/

Avoid:

/page?id=123

Avoid unnecessary dates in blog URLs.

Avoid random IDs in public URLs.

Never change an established public URL without a valid reason.


# RENDERING

Prefer:

- Server Components
- Static generation
- Server-side rendering
- ISR/revalidation when appropriate

Use Client Components only when client-side interaction is actually required.

Do NOT add "use client" unnecessarily.

Do NOT fetch MongoDB data for pages that can remain static.

Do NOT create unnecessary frontend-to-backend API requests.


# DATABASE

Use MongoDB with Mongoose.

Only create collections with a genuine purpose.

Potential collections:

- admin_users
- blog_posts
- blog_categories
- blog_authors
- contact_submissions
- case_studies

Do not create unnecessary collections.

Do not expose MongoDB directly to the browser.


# NAMING CONVENTIONS

IMPORTANT:

Database collection names MUST use snake_case.

Examples:

blog_posts
blog_categories
admin_users
contact_submissions

Database fields MUST use snake_case.

Examples:

featured_image
featured_image_alt
seo_title
seo_description
focus_keyword
published_at
created_at
updated_at

JavaScript variables MUST use snake_case.

Examples:

const blog_post = ...
const seo_title = ...
const published_at = ...

Object properties should use snake_case.

API request/response fields should use snake_case.

Functions do NOT have to use snake_case.

Functions may use camelCase.

Examples:

getBlogPost()
createBlogPost()
generateSitemap()

React components must use PascalCase.

Examples:

BlogCard
ServiceCard
HeroSection


# DESIGN

The visual direction is:

Premium technology company
+
Modern media/creative agency

Desired characteristics:

- Sleek
- Elegant
- Premium
- Editorial
- Sophisticated
- Minimal
- Strong typography
- Generous whitespace
- High-quality imagery
- Strong visual hierarchy
- Modern layouts
- Subtle micro-interactions
- Smooth scrolling

Avoid:

- Generic SaaS templates
- Excessive glassmorphism
- Excessive gradients
- Excessive glow effects
- Excessive rounded cards
- Excessive animations
- Clutter
- Template-looking layouts


# MOTION

Smooth scrolling and subtle animation are important.

However:

Performance takes priority over animation.

Prefer:

- transform
- opacity
- GPU-friendly animations

Avoid unnecessary layout animation.

Avoid excessive scroll listeners.

Avoid heavy animation libraries unless genuinely necessary.

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

Animations must not significantly hurt:

- LCP
- CLS
- INP
- Accessibility
- Mobile performance


# PERFORMANCE

Performance is a core requirement.

Always consider:

- Core Web Vitals
- LCP
- CLS
- INP
- TTFB
- Image optimization
- Font loading
- JavaScript bundle size
- Client Components
- Third-party scripts

Use Next.js image optimization appropriately.

Lazy-load appropriate below-the-fold assets.

Avoid loading unnecessary assets.

Do not add dependencies without a legitimate reason.


# RESPONSIVE DESIGN

The website must be mobile-first and fully responsive.

Test:

320px
375px
390px
414px
768px
1024px
1280px
1440px
1920px

Check:

- Navigation
- Hero
- Typography
- Cards
- Forms
- Images
- Buttons
- Spacing
- Horizontal overflow
- Animations
- Blog readability


# ACCESSIBILITY

Use semantic HTML.

Ensure:

- Correct heading hierarchy
- Accessible links
- Accessible buttons
- Form labels
- Keyboard navigation
- Visible focus states
- Appropriate color contrast
- Meaningful image alt text
- Accessible navigation

Do not use ARIA unnecessarily.


# IMAGES

Optimize images.

Use meaningful alt text.

Do not keyword-stuff alt text.

Treat decorative images as decorative when appropriate.

Do not use unnecessarily large images.

Use responsive image loading.


# SECURITY

Never expose:

- MongoDB credentials
- Authentication secrets
- API keys
- Private environment variables
- Admin credentials

Use environment variables for secrets.

Validate input server-side.

Sanitize user-generated content before rendering.

Protect admin routes.

Protect API routes.

Never rely only on client-side validation.


# ADMIN

The admin panel is only for genuinely backend-managed content.

Potential functionality:

- Admin authentication
- Dashboard
- Blog management
- Categories
- Authors
- Contact submissions
- Case studies if approved

Do NOT turn the entire website into a CMS.

Admin pages must not be indexable.


# BLOG

The blog is an important SEO system.

Blog posts should support:

- title
- slug
- excerpt
- content
- featured_image
- featured_image_alt
- author_id
- category_id
- tags
- seo_title
- seo_description
- focus_keyword
- secondary_keywords
- canonical_url
- status
- published_at
- created_at
- updated_at

Draft posts must never accidentally become publicly indexable.

Published posts should become publicly available according to the approved SEO architecture.


# CONTENT

Content must be appropriate for the US market.

Tone:

- Professional
- Clear
- Human
- Confident
- Natural
- Business-focused
- Conversion-focused

Avoid generic agency clichés.

Never invent:

- Clients
- Awards
- Testimonials
- Statistics
- Results
- Certifications


# INTERNAL LINKING

Internal links must be intentional.

Important relationships should connect:

Homepage
→ Services
→ Individual Services
→ Industries
→ Work
→ Blog
→ Contact

Blog articles should contextually link to relevant:

- Services
- Case studies
- Related articles
- Conversion pages

Do not overuse internal links.


# CODE QUALITY

Before making changes:

1. Inspect the existing project.
2. Understand the current architecture.
3. Check existing dependencies.
4. Check existing components.
5. Avoid unnecessary rewrites.

Do not overwrite working code unnecessarily.

Do not add unrelated functionality.

Do not add unnecessary dependencies.

Keep code readable and maintainable.


# PHASE DEVELOPMENT

This project will be developed one phase at a time.

When I give you a phase prompt:

ONLY implement that phase.

Do NOT implement future phases.

Do NOT create backend functionality before it is requested.

Do NOT create admin functionality before it is requested.

Do NOT create unnecessary dynamic systems.

After completing a phase, report:

1. What was implemented
2. Files created
3. Files modified
4. Dependencies added
5. SEO considerations
6. Performance considerations
7. Issues found
8. Decisions requiring approval

Do not continue to another phase without explicit instruction.

# VISUAL ASSETS & ICON USAGE

The website should use visual assets selectively.

Do NOT create a completely text-only website.

Use SOME high-quality images where they genuinely improve the design,
such as:

- Homepage hero/supporting visual
- Selected service sections
- Industry sections
- Work/case-study presentation
- Important visual storytelling sections

Do NOT use images in every section.

Do NOT overload the website with generic stock photography.

If an image is required but no approved image has been provided:

Create a clearly visible placeholder in the layout.

After completing the phase, report:

1. Required image filename
2. Directory inside /public
3. Recommended dimensions
4. Recommended aspect ratio
5. Description of the image that should be placed there
6. Where the image appears on the website

Example:

/public/images/home/hero-visual.webp
Recommended size: 1600 × 1200
Aspect ratio: 4:3
Purpose: Homepage hero supporting visual
Suggested content: Premium digital product/interface or professional
technology-focused visual.

Do NOT automatically download random internet images unless explicitly
requested.


# ICONS

Icons may be used selectively where they improve comprehension or visual
hierarchy.

Good uses include:

- Selected service features
- Process steps
- UI controls
- Feature highlights
- Contact information
- Some visual callouts

Do NOT:

- Put an icon beside every heading
- Fill every card with icons
- Use icons purely because empty space exists
- Mix multiple icon libraries

If an icon library becomes necessary, use ONE consistent lightweight
library after approval.


# ANIMATION DIRECTION

The website should contain visible but restrained professional animation.

Appropriate animation includes:

- Fade/reveal on selected sections
- Small vertical/horizontal entrance movement
- Image reveal
- Subtle hover movement
- Button/arrow interaction
- Underline/line animation
- Small decorative movement

Animation should make the website feel polished and smooth without becoming
the focus of the experience.

Do NOT animate every element.

Do NOT use heavy animation simply to make the website appear modern.

Prefer CSS transitions/animations where practical.

If an animation library becomes genuinely beneficial, request approval
before adding it.

Always respect prefers-reduced-motion.


# BEFORE CODING

For every phase:

1. Read these project rules.
2. Inspect the existing project.
3. Read relevant documentation from previous phases.
4. Briefly explain your implementation plan.
5. Implement ONLY the requested phase.
6. Test the implementation.
7. Fix errors.
8. Report the result.

If an architectural decision could significantly affect:

- SEO
- Performance
- Database structure
- Security
- Future scalability

stop and ask for approval before making that decision.